WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.300
We have moved borders many ways.

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.080
Can one idea tie the results together?

3
00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:12.783
More FGF8 at the front: the barrel field moved back.

4
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:16.233
FGF8 blocked: it moved forward.

5
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:20.433
A new source at the back: a partial second barrel field.

6
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:26.753
No Emx2: the front-and-middle areas grew back, and the visual area shrank.

7
00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:32.863
More Emx2, same slope: the visual area grew, with total size unchanged.

8
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.653
No thalamic axons: marker borders in place at birth.

9
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:44.053
New input: barrels in grafted visual tissue, and an orientation map in auditory

10
00:00:44.053 --> 00:00:45.053
cortex.

11
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:49.263
Which factor's loss and gain results did not mirror each other?

12
00:00:55.583 --> 00:01:00.943
Pax6. Losing it shifted the markers, but raising it barely moved the map.

13
00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:04.197
Here is the idea that ties them together.

14
00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.657
Position draws the sketch; input finishes it.

15
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:13.887
Each progenitor's position is recorded as levels of graded signals and transcription

16
00:01:13.887 --> 00:01:14.887
factors.

17
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:19.587
In our working model, a border falls where a level crosses a threshold.

18
00:01:20.607 --> 00:01:22.327
Change the dose, and the border slides.

19
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.420
Add a second source, and part of the pattern can repeat.

20
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:32.610
Thalamic input then sharpens the sketch, tells primary areas from higher-order ones,

21
00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.570
and helps shape the maps inside.

22
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:40.710
And the strongest evidence for a factor: loss and gain that move borders in opposite

23
00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:45.170
directions. A missing converse result does not rule a factor out.

24
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:47.393
The rule has limits.

25
00:01:48.273 --> 00:01:51.993
It covers where the primary area borders sit, front to back, in the mouse.

26
00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:57.713
It does not cover total cortical growth, which some of these factors also change.

27
00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:02.677
Its threshold model does not explain the maps inside an area.

28
00:02:03.777 --> 00:02:06.777
Input and activity build those with the cortex's own cells.

29
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.280
it is drawn from mice.

30
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.580
It does not claim to explain area sizes in primates or humans.

31
00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:16.293
One question stays open.

32
00:02:17.113 --> 00:02:19.913
A gradient is smooth, yet mature borders are sharp.

33
00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:23.493
How does the smooth gradient become a sharp border?

34
00:02:24.373 --> 00:02:28.253
And how much happens in progenitors, and how much in neurons under input?

35
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:33.163
The takeaway: position draws the sketch, and input finishes it.

36
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:37.123
How the sketch becomes a sharp line is not yet fully understood.
